{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "45",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 50s with stage IV B-RAF V600E mutant malignant melanoma with metastases of the liver, lungs, and peritoneum (primary site unknown). Within 2 weeks of starting ipilimumab treatment, she developed skin symptoms. After initial improvement and later disease recurrence, she received a second course of ipilimumab 14 months later, which led to recurrence and worsening of symptoms.\n\nPhysical findings included:- Photodistributed erythematous to violaceous eruption- Diffuse erythema of forehead and midface, involving nasolabial folds- Erythema and edema of upper eyelids- Diffuse erythema of upper chest and upper back- Flagellate erythema on posterior neck- Flat-topped pink to erythematous papules over knuckles- Prominent erythema at nailfolds with dilated capillary loops, capillary dropout, and ragged cuticles- Grade 4/5 strength on resisted abduction of shoulders bilaterally- Limited flexion of both hips- Reduced activity level due to weakness and early fatigue\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Creatine kinase: 1088 U/L (increased to 1854 U/L)- Aldolase: 23.0 U/L- C-reactive protein: 28.6 mg/L- Antinuclear antibody titer: 1:640 with speckled pattern- Jo-1 antibody: negativeImaging Studies:- MRI showed mild short TI inversion recovery hyperintensity of bilateral vastus lateralis and rectus femoris musclesBiopsy Results:- Vastus lateralis muscle biopsy showed atrophy of type II muscle fibers without evidence of inflammatory myositis (note: performed after corticosteroid treatment)",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Progressive Multisystem Syndrome with Dermatological and Muscular Involvement",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
